  data:  # fb5e
    kind: work_item  # fb5e
    title: "rust-analyzer opt-in nudge fires even when the backend is enabled and running"  # fb5e
    status: todo_hard  # fb5e
    priority: 3  # fb5e
    parent:  # fb5e
    tags:  # fb5e
    - rust_analyzer  # fb5e
    - developer_experience  # fb5e
    isbefore: []  # fb5e
    duplicate_of: []  # fb5e
    not_duplicate_of: []  # fb5e
    pr_ref:  # fb5e
    description: "`check_rust_analyzer_disclosure` decides between two messages on `available`\n(is the binary on PATH) alone, after a single guard on \"does this repo contain\nRust\". It never consults the opt-in gate, so on any Rust repo where the binary\nis installed it prints the \"NOT enabled\" arm regardless of whether the backend\nis enabled and running.\n\nREPRO (2026-08-18, this box, rust-analyzer at ~/.cargo/bin/rust-analyzer):\n\n    cd <a rust crate>\n    hypergumbo survey . --backend rust-analyzer --out /tmp/ra.json\n\nObserved on stderr:\n\n    UserWarning: rust-analyzer is installed but NOT enabled. It resolves Rust\n    types and call targets far more precisely than the built-in tree-sitter\n    analyzer.\n      Enable per run:  --backend rust-analyzer\n      Or by env:       HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1\n\nThe backend WAS enabled and DID run in that same invocation. Positive control\nfrom the emitted artifact: /tmp/ra.json carries 2 edges of type `references`\nwith `origin == [\"scip\"]`, which only the SCIP path produces; the tree-sitter\nrun of the same crate has zero. So the message is not merely redundant, it\ncontradicts observable behaviour in the run that printed it.\n\nThe counterfactual matters for the fix: the warning is CORRECT and wanted on\nthe default (tree-sitter) run of a Rust repo with the binary present. Only the\nenabled case is wrong. A fix that suppresses the whole warning would delete a\ndeliberate disclosure -- the function's docstring is explicit that the\nbuild.rs / proc-macro execution caveat must reach the user BEFORE they opt in.\n\nEXPECTATION: when the backend is enabled for this run, either say nothing or\nsay something true (e.g. confirm it is active, with the same trust caveat).\nThe \"here is how to enable it\" arm must fire only when it is not enabled.\n\nSCOPE: cosmetic-but-corrosive. It trains users to ignore a warning whose whole\npurpose is to carry a security-relevant disclosure about executing untrusted\nbuild scripts.\n\nFound while empirically checking a stale spec claim in §\"Taint flow analysis\"\nabout the rust-analyzer backend and the (now-closed) tree-sitter Rust\nmodule_attr_ref gap."  # fb5e
    fields: {}  # fb5e
